00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:22.570
Archimedes was the ancient Greek inventor
and mathematician who is remembered for being

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:26.060
one of the greatest minds in all of human
history.

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:31.509
His theorems became laws of physics, and his
equations became essential for future generations

00:00:31.509 --> 00:00:33.500
of scientists and engineers.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:37.720
While his great passion in life was the study
geometry, Archimedes also used his mind to

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:42.580
invent weapons of war that successfully defended
the city of Syracuse from Roman invaders.

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:47.750
On today’s Biographic, we tell the story
of a man whose legacy has lasted thousands

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:49.360
of years.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:54.829
A Life of Academic Achievement

00:00:54.829 --> 00:00:59.920
Archimedes was born in 287 BC in the city
of Syracuse, which is located on the east

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:01.670
coast of the island of Sicily.

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:05.220
He was the son of Phidias, who was a renowned
mathematician and astronomer.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:10.510
When he completed his basic education in Sicily,
Archimedes traveled to Egypt to complete his

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:11.510
education.

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:15.830
At the time, the city of Alexandria was known
for being one of the greatest places to receive

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:17.960
an education in the entire world.

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:22.310
Of course, not everyone can afford to send
their child to Egypt for school, which leads

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:25.450
people to believe that he may have been descended
from nobility.

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:31.040
Many scholars claim that he was a relative
of King Hieron II, who was the ruler of Syracuse

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:32.270
at that time.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:36.250
When Archimedes returned to Syracuse after
completing his education, he arrived with

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:39.810
the object that is credited as one of his
first inventions.

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:42.460
This was known as the “Archimedes Screw”.

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:45.500
Scholars believe that Archimedes could have
borrowed the idea from something that had

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:49.980
already existed in Egypt, but bringing it
to Greece it truly revolutionized life for

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:51.229
people living in Syracuse.

00:01:51.229 --> 00:01:55.560
This was a spiral-shaped device that could
be placed into a river or a ditch of water,

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:58.290
and when you turn the handle, water travels
uphill.

00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:02.950
This made it incredibly easy to fill a bucket
with water, and to drain an underground well

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:03.950
or ditch.

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:05.229
The device is now named after him, called
the Archimedes Screw.

00:02:05.229 --> 00:02:08.700
It would later be used to water the Hanging
Gardens of Babylon, which became one of the

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:10.590
Seven Wonders of the Ancient World.

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:14.730
The Archimedes Screw is so efficient, it is
still sometimes to irrigate fields to this

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:15.980
very day.

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:20.060
Aside from these details of his achievements,
we don’t know very much about the personal

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:21.290
life of Archimedes.

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:25.340
He actually did give the details of his life
to his friend and biographer, Heracleides,

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:27.819
but the text was lost over the course of time.

00:02:27.819 --> 00:02:31.210
We don’t even know if he was ever married,
or had any children.

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:35.470
But the stories of his greatest achievements
were based down through the years, which is

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:37.320
how they still survived today.

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:41.750
Many of these stories seem so outlandish,
that they are often debated, and some historians

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:43.730
consider to be legends.

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:46.569
At the very least, we do know Archimedes’
personality.

00:02:46.569 --> 00:02:50.901
According to the Greek biographer Plutarch,
Archimedes was so hyper-focused on his work,

00:02:50.901 --> 00:02:53.310
that it became the most important thing in
his life.

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:56.209
He often forgot to take cadre of his basic
human needs.

00:02:56.209 --> 00:02:59.340
"(Archimedes would) forget his food and neglect
his person, to the degree that when he was

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:03.250
occasionally carried by absolute violence
to bathe or have his body anointed.

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:08.069
He used to trace geometrical figures in the
ashes of the fire, and diagrams in the oil

00:03:08.069 --> 00:03:09.069
on his body.

00:03:09.069 --> 00:03:14.890
(He was) in a state of entire preoccupation
and, in the truest sense, divine possession

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:17.099
with his love and delight in science."

00:03:17.099 --> 00:03:23.410
The Golden Grown

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:27.120
One of the most famous stories about Archimedes
is that of the golden crown.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:31.690
This story was originally recorded during
the first century BC by a Roman architect

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:36.650
named Vitruvius, based on the tale that had
been passed down for generations by word-of-mouth.

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:39.620
It has been retold again and again ever since.

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:44.489
As the story goes, a local goldsmith in Syracuse
was commissioned to create a golden wreath

00:03:44.489 --> 00:03:46.480
crown out of pure gold.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:50.970
These crowns were placed on the heads of the
statues of the Greek Gods and Goddesses in

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:52.470
order to pay tribute to them.

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:56.170
However, once King Hieron got the crown back,
he was surprised by its weight.

00:03:56.170 --> 00:04:01.180
He believed that the goldsmith possibly swindled
him out of some of his gold by creating the

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:04.590
crown by using silver, and then plating it
with gold.

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:08.180
However, he had no way to prove that without
damaging the crown.

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:12.380
Archimedes was given the task of figuring
out of a golden crown was truly made of pure

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:15.430
gold, or if it was simply a gold-plating on
a cheaper metal.

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:19.829
After he was given this problem to solve,
he became obsessed with it, and this was all

00:04:19.829 --> 00:04:21.799
that he could think of day and night.

00:04:21.799 --> 00:04:24.919
He would write down mathematical equations
without positive results.

00:04:24.919 --> 00:04:29.400
Just like always, he stopped eating, sleeping,
and bathing while he tried to solve the problem.

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:33.240
Eventually, his students dragged him into
the bath house to clean himself, because he

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:35.289
was beginning to seriously reek.

00:04:35.289 --> 00:04:38.770
When he laid down in the bath, some of the
water splashed over the edge of the tub.

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:43.050
He realized that the weight of his body dispersed
the water, and the excess had to leave the

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:44.849
tub in order to accommodate his body.

00:04:44.849 --> 00:04:48.830
Suddenly, he realized that he could use this
to solve the issue of the golden crown.

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:53.520
Apparently, he was so excited by this discovery
that he jumped out of the bathtub, and ran

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:57.830
down the streets shouting, “Eureka!”-
“I have found it!” without ever bothering

00:04:57.830 --> 00:04:58.949
to get dressed.

00:04:58.949 --> 00:05:03.439
People gasped and covered their children’s
eyes, while others laughed at the eccentric

00:05:03.439 --> 00:05:05.379
genius they all knew and loved.

00:05:05.379 --> 00:05:09.069
Some historians believe that this last part
was exaggerated, and that it may have just

00:05:09.069 --> 00:05:11.759
been a silly story told for fun.

00:05:11.759 --> 00:05:15.969
Even so, the practice of shouting the phrase
“Eureka!” after a huge discovery is still

00:05:15.969 --> 00:05:16.969
used today.

00:05:16.969 --> 00:05:21.759
Vitruvius wrote, “The solution which occurred
when he stepped into his bath and caused it

00:05:21.759 --> 00:05:27.099
to overflow was to put a weight of gold equal
to the crown, and known to be pure, into a

00:05:27.099 --> 00:05:30.009
bowl which was filled with water to the brim.

00:05:30.009 --> 00:05:33.249
Then the gold would be removed and the king’s
crown put in, in its place.

00:05:33.249 --> 00:05:37.130
An alloy of lighter silver would increase
the bulk of the crown and cause the bowl to

00:05:37.130 --> 00:05:38.130
overflow.”

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:41.939
Once Archimedes proposed this solution to
the king, they compared the containers of

00:05:41.939 --> 00:05:46.869
water side-by-side, it was clear that the
goldsmith had, in fact, taken a lot of the

00:05:46.869 --> 00:05:50.159
gold for himself, and gave the king a gold-plated
crown.

00:05:50.159 --> 00:05:53.659
They had sufficient evidence to find him guilty
of stealing from the king.

00:05:53.659 --> 00:05:56.949
In modern times, there are some scholars who
believe that this story has been over-simplified

00:05:56.949 --> 00:06:00.909
throughout the years, and that Achimedes may
have used a more complex method in order to

00:06:00.909 --> 00:06:03.689
figure out the solution to the golden crown.

00:06:03.689 --> 00:06:08.339
Others say that it was a symbolic story, and
that the incident never happened at all.

00:06:08.339 --> 00:06:11.649
But we do know that it truly did happen, because
these experiments resulted in the creation

00:06:11.649 --> 00:06:13.279
of the Archimedes Principle.

00:06:13.279 --> 00:06:16.899
This discovery would later become a law of
physics, and began as the basics of fluid

00:06:16.899 --> 00:06:17.899
mechanics.

00:06:17.899 --> 00:06:19.529
It is still used by engineers today.

