00:07:32.630 --> 00:07:37.639
Inventions and Achievements

00:07:37.639 --> 00:07:41.849
Throughout his lifetime, King Hieron II continued
to bring his requests to Archimedes, and he

00:07:41.849 --> 00:07:45.199
was often challenged to use his mind to create
the impossible.

00:07:45.199 --> 00:07:48.919
The King commissioned him to build a vessel
known as The Syracusia.

00:07:48.919 --> 00:07:53.260
It was said to have been one of the largest
boats that was ever built in ancient times,

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:58.120
and required the same amount of material that
would normally go into building 60 ships.

00:07:58.120 --> 00:08:02.029
It took over 100 men to assemble it after
one year of work.

00:08:02.029 --> 00:08:07.479
According to the historian Moschion of Phaselis,
the interior of the Syracusia was so extravagant,

00:08:07.479 --> 00:08:09.659
it would even impress people in modern times.

00:08:09.659 --> 00:08:11.750
The boat was to be constructed of exotic wood
and marble.

00:08:11.750 --> 00:08:16.469
It had watchtowers staffed with soldiers,
statues of the gods, fresh water baths, a

00:08:16.469 --> 00:08:20.129
massive dining hall serving the finest food,
a gymnasium, a library, and a temple.

00:08:20.129 --> 00:08:22.039
Objects were made of ivory and gold.

00:08:22.039 --> 00:08:25.069
One of the largest pieces of original artwork
that was commissioned for the ship was an

00:08:25.069 --> 00:08:29.459
illustration of the entire epic of the Iliad
along the walls of the ship that had been

00:08:29.459 --> 00:08:31.569
hand-assembled in mosaic tiles.

00:08:31.569 --> 00:08:35.030
King Hieron wanted to do more than just a
luxury pleasure cruise along the coast line.

00:08:35.030 --> 00:08:38.030
He planned to take it across the Mediterranian
sea.

00:08:38.030 --> 00:08:41.210
So, how could something so heavy actually
float?

00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:45.580
Archimedes figured out The Law of Buoyancy
and hydrostatics in order to make it all work.

00:08:45.580 --> 00:08:49.840
The King wanted the impossible, and yet Archimedes
was able to make it happen yet again.

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:54.800
Without his calculations and the Law of Buoyancy,
modern-day cruise ships and mega-yachts may

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:55.970
not exist.

00:08:55.970 --> 00:09:00.280
Once the ship was finally finished, the people
of Syracuse celebrated its completion, and

00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:08.080
nearly 2,000 people were allowed on board.

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:18.060
tHowever, after its maiden voyage from Sicily
to Egypt, no one knows what happened to the

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:19.060
famous ship.

00:09:19.060 --> 00:09:22.560
Aside from figuring out how luxury cruise
liners can float, Archimedes invented some

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:26.891
pretty incredible things that we still use
to this very day, like the Law of the Lever

00:09:26.891 --> 00:09:27.920
and Balance.

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:31.410
He told the King that he could lift any object,
no matter how heavy it was.

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:35.740
He was quoted saying to the King, “Give
me a place to stand, and I will move the Earth”.

00:09:35.740 --> 00:09:39.450
Impressed and slightly skeptical, the King
asked to see him lift a ship out of the sea.

00:09:39.450 --> 00:09:44.220
Through a set of cogs and pulleys, Archimedes
was able to sit down at one end of the lever

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:47.090
and lift a ship that was full of heavy gear.

00:09:47.090 --> 00:09:51.120
While we take them for granted today, his
simple machine inventions of the level, pulley,

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:55.900
and screw were completely revolutionary, and
would be essential to building modern society.

00:09:55.900 --> 00:10:00.180
All of this was incredibly impressive, but
his favorite achievements might surprise you.

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:02.090
They were his geometric equations.

00:10:02.090 --> 00:10:06.670
In one of his publications called Method,
he explained how he was testing various levers

00:10:06.670 --> 00:10:08.040
with geometric shapes.

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:12.240
He would see how weights balanced against
one another with the center of gravity.

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:15.810
Sometimes, he would see that something worked,
but that wasn’t enough.

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:19.610
He needed to use math to prove step-by-step
why it worked.

00:10:19.610 --> 00:10:22.460
These were some of the world’s first geometric
proofs.

00:10:22.460 --> 00:10:26.360
Archimedes was so obsessed with calculating
math problems, that he would carry around

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:29.860
a tray of sand with him so that he could trace
calculations with his fingers.

00:10:29.860 --> 00:10:33.670
One of his most significant contributions
to math came from his publication called On

00:10:33.670 --> 00:10:37.820
the Measurement of the Circle, where he figured
out the ratio of a circle's circumference

00:10:37.820 --> 00:10:42.150
to its diameter is Pi, or 3.14.

00:10:42.150 --> 00:10:46.170
He worked on calculating the volume and surface
area of various shapes and objects.

00:10:46.170 --> 00:10:50.570
He even created the system of using exponents
to describe large numbers.

00:10:50.570 --> 00:10:54.400
Before this time, Greek scientists would perform
an experiment, and draw conclusions from it

00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:55.400
afterwards.

00:10:55.400 --> 00:10:59.260
Archimedes would begin with math first, and
then perform experiments in order to attempt

00:10:59.260 --> 00:11:01.170
to see what would happen in reality.

00:11:01.170 --> 00:11:05.250
Through this form of experimentation, Archimedes
was responsible for so many theorems that

00:11:05.250 --> 00:11:08.170
would eventually be classified as laws of
physics.

00:11:08.170 --> 00:11:11.850
Because of this, his mind has been compared
to Einstein.

00:11:11.850 --> 00:11:17.650
His brain was a truly remarkable level of
genius that you rarely see but once in a lifetime.

00:11:17.650 --> 00:11:23.150
The Roman Invasion of Syracuse

00:11:23.150 --> 00:11:27.480
In the 3rd century BC, the Mediterranean basin
was split between the Carthaginians in the

00:11:27.480 --> 00:11:29.310
west, while the Greeks in the east.

00:11:29.310 --> 00:11:33.450
The Romans controlled Rome, of course, but
they were getting ready to invade the surrounding

00:11:33.450 --> 00:11:34.980
territory to build their empire.

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:40.490
The Romans and the Carthaginians fought the
First Punic War from 264 to 241 BC.

00:11:40.490 --> 00:11:43.870
The war ended with Rome taking the victory
over Carthage.

00:11:43.870 --> 00:11:46.450
This meant that Sicily was now a Roman territory.

00:11:46.450 --> 00:11:51.440
King Hieron II was able to maintain peace
with Rome, and remain and independent city,

00:11:51.440 --> 00:11:54.310
so long as they paid tribute in the form of
goods.

00:11:54.310 --> 00:11:59.590
This all changed in 218BC when The Second
Punic War began.

00:11:59.590 --> 00:12:05.060
In 215 BC, King Hieron II died, and King Hieronymus
became the new ruler of Syracuse.

00:12:05.060 --> 00:12:09.940
He chose to switch the city’s loyalty from
Rome, and became rivals with Carthage instead.

00:12:09.940 --> 00:12:11.610
Clearly, this was a huge mistake.

00:12:11.610 --> 00:12:17.340
From 214 to 212 BC, the city of Syracuse now
had to fend off Roman invaders.

00:12:17.340 --> 00:12:21.270
So far in this story, you may think that Archimedes
was more of a nerdy type who was only concerned

00:12:21.270 --> 00:12:22.270
with maths.

00:12:22.270 --> 00:12:25.230
If you were to put him in a fight, you wouldn’t
exactly put your bets on him that he would

00:12:25.230 --> 00:12:26.230
win.

00:12:26.230 --> 00:12:29.730
But when the king gave him the task of inventing
unstoppable war machines to fight the Roman

00:12:29.730 --> 00:12:32.410
Empire, his mind delivered.

00:12:32.410 --> 00:12:36.740
Archimedes may have been an old man in his
70’s, but his mind was still sharp as a

00:12:36.740 --> 00:12:37.740
tack.

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:39.180
Yet again, he was able to achieve the impossible.

00:12:39.180 --> 00:12:43.960
He created defense weapons that successfully
defended the city for at least two years.

00:12:43.960 --> 00:12:47.370
One of his most powerful weapons was known
as The Archimedes Claw.

00:12:47.370 --> 00:12:49.100
The claw was attached to his levels and pulley
system.

00:12:49.100 --> 00:12:53.430
The claw would grab on to the front of a Roman
boat, lift it up in the air, and drop it back

00:12:53.430 --> 00:12:54.430
into the sea.

00:12:54.430 --> 00:12:58.200
This would capsize the ship, dumping the crew
and all of its weapons into the Medeterranian

00:12:58.200 --> 00:12:59.200
sea.

00:12:59.200 --> 00:13:02.490
The Roman commander, Marcellus was quoted
saying, “Archimedes uses my ships to ladle

00:13:02.490 --> 00:13:04.560
sea water into his wine cups”

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:06.150
But he didn’t stop there.

00:13:06.150 --> 00:13:10.570
Archimedes also created both large and small
catapults that were able to blast holes through

00:13:10.570 --> 00:13:11.670
the Roman ships.

00:13:11.670 --> 00:13:17.000
According to Plutarch, "When Archimedes began
to ply his engines, he at once shot against

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:22.010
the land forces all sorts of missile weapons,
and immense masses of stone that came down

00:13:22.010 --> 00:13:25.820
with incredible noise and violence; against
which no man could stand; for they knocked

00:13:25.820 --> 00:13:31.520
down those upon whom they fell in heaps, breaking
all their ranks and files.”

00:13:31.520 --> 00:13:36.110
Archimedes has also been credited for helping
Syracuse use “burning mirrors”, which

00:13:36.110 --> 00:13:40.400
reflected the sunlight onto the sails of the
Roman ships, causing them to catch fire.

00:13:40.400 --> 00:13:44.100
The following scene was described in a 12th
Century text called The Book of Histories:

00:13:44.100 --> 00:13:48.490
“The old man [Archimedes] constructed a
kind of hexagonal mirror, and at an interval

00:13:48.490 --> 00:13:52.900
proportionate to the size of the mirror he
set similar small mirrors with four edges,

00:13:52.900 --> 00:13:58.980
moved by links and by a form of hinge, and
made it the centre of the sun's beams--its

00:13:58.980 --> 00:14:01.890
noon-tide beam, whether in summer or in mid-winter.

00:14:01.890 --> 00:14:06.430
Afterwards, when the beams were reflected
in the mirror, a fearful kindling of fire

00:14:06.430 --> 00:14:11.670
was raised in the ships, and at the distance
of a bow-shot he turned them into ashes.”

00:14:11.670 --> 00:14:15.520
The defense weapons were so successful, that
they were responsible for killing thousands

00:14:15.520 --> 00:14:16.550
of Roman soldiers.

00:14:16.550 --> 00:14:21.110
So long as the continued to go after Syracuse,
the men were killed by these machines.

00:14:21.110 --> 00:14:25.670
Commander Marcus Claudius Marcellus decided
that it was futile to attack Syracuse, so

00:14:25.670 --> 00:14:29.680
they waited until they could find their perfect
moment to conquer the city.

00:14:29.680 --> 00:14:33.380
By 212 BC, Syracuse could not defend itself
for much longer.

00:14:33.380 --> 00:14:37.140
According to some historians, the citizens
of the city were beginning to starve, because

00:14:37.140 --> 00:14:40.390
they were surrounded by the Romans and could
not get their supplies.

00:14:40.390 --> 00:14:42.300
This starvation forced them to surrender.

00:14:42.300 --> 00:14:46.670
According to others, Syracuse refused to surrender,
and Archimedes’ weapons were strong enough

00:14:46.670 --> 00:14:47.670
to hold them off.

00:14:47.670 --> 00:14:51.720
But the Romans managed to take advantage of
a weak spot in one of their land-facing defensive

00:14:51.720 --> 00:14:54.930
walls while everyone was busy celebrating
a religious festival.

00:14:54.930 --> 00:15:02.000
Either way, Rome finally captured Syracuse,
and ultimately won the Second Punic War.

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:06.150
Death, and Legacy

00:15:06.150 --> 00:15:10.890
Marcellus Claudius Marcellus knew that Archimedes
was responsible for these glorious weapons,

00:15:10.890 --> 00:15:14.320
and he knew that he could be an asset to the
Roman Empire.

00:15:14.320 --> 00:15:18.680
Once they captured the city, he gave strict
orders that this genius was an asset, and

00:15:18.680 --> 00:15:19.800
must not be harmed.

00:15:19.800 --> 00:15:21.940
He gave the order to bring Archimedes to him.

00:15:21.940 --> 00:15:25.830
The only issue was that the Roman soldiers
had no idea what Archimedes looked like.

00:15:25.830 --> 00:15:29.540
The finer details of the story of his death
change depending on the source, but what we

00:15:29.540 --> 00:15:34.360
do know is that Archimedes was in the middle
of working on his geometry on the beach.

00:15:34.360 --> 00:15:37.920
He was tracing images of circles and mathematical
equations in the sand.

00:15:37.920 --> 00:15:42.700
A Roman warrior walked over top of his geometric
drawings, which scattered all of his work.

00:15:42.700 --> 00:15:47.140
Archimedes was outraged, and seemed to be
totally oblivious to the fact that this was

00:15:47.140 --> 00:15:48.140
a Roman soldier.

00:15:48.140 --> 00:15:52.760
According to legend, his last words were,
“Do not disturb my circles!” before being

00:15:52.760 --> 00:15:54.490
cut down by the man’s sword.

00:15:54.490 --> 00:15:56.290
He was 75 years old.

00:15:56.290 --> 00:16:00.510
After his death, Archimedes was buried in
a large tomb that represented his mathematical

00:16:00.510 --> 00:16:01.510
achievements.

00:16:01.510 --> 00:16:04.730
Even though he had destroyed so many Roman
ships, they still had a tremendous amount

00:16:04.730 --> 00:16:08.510
of respect for him, and scholars would visit
to pay homage to his great mind.

00:16:08.510 --> 00:16:12.010
At the tomb, there was a statue of a sphere
and a cylinder.

00:16:12.010 --> 00:16:16.339
This represented what Archimedes considered
to be his greatest achievement; “If a sphere

00:16:16.339 --> 00:16:22.900
is inscribed in a cylinder, then the sphere
is 2/3 of the cylinder in both surface area

00:16:22.900 --> 00:16:24.029
and volume.”

00:16:24.029 --> 00:16:28.470
Years later, the famous Roman statesman named
Cicero wrote that he wanted to pay his respects

00:16:28.470 --> 00:16:29.930
to the tomb of Archimedes.

00:16:29.930 --> 00:16:33.020
After searching for hours, he finally found
it near the Agrigentine gate in Syracuse.

00:16:33.020 --> 00:16:36.950
It was overgrown with vegetation, and clear
that no one had bothered to visit in a very

00:16:36.950 --> 00:16:37.950
long time.

00:16:37.950 --> 00:16:41.920
Cicero cleaned up the area, and wrote down
the inscriptions that he found on the tomb,

00:16:41.920 --> 00:16:44.020
so that it would never be forgotten by history.

00:16:44.020 --> 00:16:48.221
In modern times, people have theories as to
where the location of the tomb may be, but

00:16:48.221 --> 00:16:49.890
no one knows for sure.

00:16:49.890 --> 00:16:53.550
In the 1960’s, the Hotel Panorama claimed
that they discovered the tomb of Archimedes

00:16:53.550 --> 00:16:56.770
during construction, and that they preserved
it in their court yard.

00:16:56.770 --> 00:17:01.020
However, some people believe that this was
just a publicity stunt to attract more guests

00:17:01.020 --> 00:17:02.020
to the hotel.

00:17:02.020 --> 00:17:06.130
Years after his death, the works of Archimedes
had been re-written, passed down by scribes

00:17:06.130 --> 00:17:07.199
over and over again.

00:17:07.199 --> 00:17:11.100
As tragic as it sounds, there must be countless
numbers of notes and texts that Archimedes

00:17:11.100 --> 00:17:15.959
created from his lifetime of work, and it
would have been lost or destroyed over time.

00:17:15.959 --> 00:17:19.639
Even though it was nearly impossible to find
one of these missing original texts, it happened

00:17:19.639 --> 00:17:20.639
In 1906.

00:17:20.639 --> 00:17:25.769
A lost work known as the Archimedes Palimpsest
were discovered buried in an old Christian

00:17:25.769 --> 00:17:26.769
text.

00:17:26.769 --> 00:17:31.710
Way back in 1229 AD, a man named Johannes
Myronas was reusing parchment from old books,

00:17:31.710 --> 00:17:35.840
and he unwittingly covered up the original
work of Archimedes and replaced it with prayers.

00:17:35.840 --> 00:17:41.259
In 2008, the text was purchased by its present
owner who was open to the idea of allowing

00:17:41.259 --> 00:17:43.340
it to be dissected for research purposes.

00:17:43.340 --> 00:17:47.240
A group called The Friends of Archimedes got
together to restore the 3rd Codex.

00:17:47.240 --> 00:17:51.779
A team from a variety of different professional
backgrounds spent over 4 years restoring the

00:17:51.779 --> 00:17:52.779
manuscript.

00:17:52.779 --> 00:17:55.759
One of the scholars, William Noel, explains
in his TED Talk that they had to go through

00:17:55.759 --> 00:18:00.619
a painstaking process of removing glue and
examining the pages one-by-one under various

00:18:00.619 --> 00:18:03.580
light sources in order to reveal the hidden
works of Archimedes.

00:18:03.580 --> 00:18:07.539
This group did not do this project for the
money, but rather, for the love of knowledge,

00:18:07.539 --> 00:18:10.389
and out of respect for Archimedes’ mind.

00:18:10.389 --> 00:18:14.210
They believed that this information should
be free to the public, so they made the entire

00:18:14.210 --> 00:18:16.590
3rd Codex available to read online for free.

00:18:16.590 --> 00:18:20.630
The only catch is that you need to know how
to read in ancient Greek.

00:18:20.630 --> 00:18:23.019
The odds that we will ever find more of his
discoveries like this are slim-to-none.

00:18:23.019 --> 00:18:27.440
But the work that we do remember from Archimedes
was enough to revolutionize modern-day math,

00:18:27.440 --> 00:18:28.830
science, and engineering.

00:18:28.830 --> 00:18:47.229
Without his brilliant mind, the world truly
would be a very different place.

